Dear Murray,

Thank you very much for all of your help, it has been very useful.

I just wanted to follow-up on this particular point, from the first messages sent (My question is in gray and your answer is in blue):

6. When using a 2-class mixture model (h2), is it possible to identify which marked individuals were attributed to each group? I would like to explore if sigma varies spatially, i.e. if sigma is different for wild dogs close to communities vs wild dogs far from communities. I do not see how I could explore this idea other than introducing the covariate h2 (but at the moment, although the model seems to work, I cannot tell what these two groups represent). If this is not possible, would there be an alternative way to test this hypothesis?

6. 'secr' doesn't do this (note lower case). I included a function classMembership in openCR that does this for the different set of models in that package. The procedure is to estimate the likelihood components for each animal given membership in each class and assign a proportional probability of membership. Although in principle it could be implemented in 'secr' the coding is very different so it won't happen soon. On the other hand, you should not expect that the classes align with biological groups - they needn't 'represent' anything other than variability in the parameter. One might address your hypothesis of spatial variation in sigma via non-euclidean distance models, but I'm not going to push that as I foresee other complications and the signs are you have limited data..

What are your reasons for suggesting to not use non-euclidean distance models and what complications do you foresee? Are the complications related to the use of multi-sessions, mark-resight models with this approach?

Thanks!

Vanessa

