Hi Murray,

Thanks for your answer.

I am trying to fit a model, in which sigma and density can vary according to a two-class habitat covariate (which I already included into the habitatmask, called ‘Community’). Following the non-euclidean secr vignette, I seem to understand that example #5 corresponds exactly to my situation. Is that correct?

I used the function fn2 from your vignette:

fn2 <- function (xy1, xy2, mask) {

if (missing(xy1)) return("noneuc")

xy1 <- addCovariates(xy1, mask)

xy2 <- addCovariates(xy2, mask)

sig1 <- as.numeric(covariates(xy1)$noneuc) # sigma(x,y) at detectors

sig2 <- as.numeric(covariates(xy2)$noneuc) # sigma(x,y) at mask points

euc <- edist(xy1, xy2)

sig <- outer (sig1, sig2, FUN = function(s1, s2) (s1 + s2)/2)

euc / sig

}

And used the following code for fitting my model:

fit.HN_e2_DHab_SigHab <- secr.fit(MRCH_e2, model = list(D~ Community,  noneuc ~ Community), mask=clippedmask_e2_cov, detectfn = 'HN', trace = FALSE, details = list(autoini = 2, knownmarks = FALSE, userdist = fn2), fixed = list(sigma = 1))

predict(fit.HN_e2_DHab_SigHab, all.levels= TRUE)

After only a few seconds of process, I obtain NA results for all estimates. The same problem occurs if I only make sigma vary with community. I was therefore wondering if this meant that the model did not converge, or if my approach or code was incorrect.

Also, can I compare AIC values between models based on non-euclidean distances with the other standard models?

Thanks again!

Vanessa

